{"new_title":"Create a Minimal Apache Struts 2.5 Web App with Java and JSP","new_heading":"Step by step Struts 2.5 tutorial for Java MVC apps and JSP views","new_body":"<h2>Welcome to Struts 2.5 and mild developer humiliation</h2>
<p>If you ever wanted to build a tiny Java MVC app and remind yourself why frameworks exist to save you time and also confuse you a little this guide is for you. We will set up a minimal Apache Struts 2.5 project add the needed dependencies wire up the Struts filter map a simple action and render a JSP view. No ceremony no magic just the parts that make a request turn into HTML.</p>

<h2>Project setup and dependencies</h2>
<p>Start with a Maven or Gradle web project. Pick a sane group and artifact so your package name does not read like a ransom note. Use packaging war if you plan to deploy to Tomcat or any servlet container that expects a war file.</p>
<p>For Maven add the main Struts artifacts and a logging library. The build tool will fetch transitive jars so you can stop playing jar Jenga.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.struts&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;struts2-core&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;2.5.x&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.struts&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;struts2-jsp-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;2.5.x&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.slf4j&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;slf4j-api&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;1.7.x&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>

<h2>Configure web xml and Struts config</h2>
<p>Register the Struts filter in web xml so requests get handed to the framework. Minimal filter and mapping fragments look like this.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;filter&gt;
  &lt;filter-name&gt;struts2&lt;/filter-name&gt;
  &lt;filter-class&gt;org.apache.struts2.dispatcher.filter.StrutsPrepareAndExecuteFilter&lt;/filter-class&gt;
&lt;/filter&gt;
&lt;filter-mapping&gt;
  &lt;filter-name&gt;struts2&lt;/filter-name&gt;
  &lt;url-pattern&gt;/*&lt;/url-pattern&gt;
&lt;/filter-mapping&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Then add a minimal struts xml package and action mapping so the framework knows which action to call for a given URL.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;struts&gt;
  &lt;package name="default" namespace="/" extends="struts-default"&gt;
    &lt;action name="hello" class="com.example.action.HelloAction"&gt;
      &lt;result name="success"&gt;/hello.jsp&lt;/result&gt;
    &lt;/action&gt;
  &lt;/package&gt;
&lt;/struts&gt;</code></pre>

<h2>Write the Action class</h2>
<p>Keep the demo action tiny. Use ActionSupport or implement Action and return the logical result name. This is not the place for business logic or life decisions.</p>
<pre><code>package com.example.action

import com.opensymphony.xwork2.ActionSupport

public class HelloAction extends ActionSupport {
  public String execute() {
    // populate any model data here
    message = "Hello from Struts"
    return SUCCESS
  }

  private String message
  public String getMessage() {
    return message
  }
}</code></pre>

<h2>Create the JSP view</h2>
<p>Keep the JSP simple for the demo. You can use Struts tags in real apps but a plain JSP with expression language is fine for testing.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Hello Struts&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Demo page&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Message from action or request attribute&lt;br&gt;${message}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>

<h2>Run the app and debugging tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Deploy the war to Tomcat or run with your IDE built in server</li>
  <li>Visit the mapped URL such as context root slash hello to see the result</li>
  <li>Enable dev mode during development by setting struts dot devMode to true in struts xml or constants to get clearer error messages and less guessing</li>
</ul>

<h3>What you just did and why it matters</h3>
<p>You created a project added Struts 2.5 dependencies wired the Struts filter configured an action mapping implemented a simple action and rendered a JSP view. In other words you built the minimal Java MVC pipeline so a browser request becomes a Java method call and then HTML. It is boring and useful which is the whole point of frameworks.</p>

<p>Now go drink coffee or refactor that messy action into something testable. If something breaks check the logs the filter mapping and the action class names before you start blaming the framework.</p>"}

{"new_title":"Master Struts2 Form Handling With Annotation Based Action Mapping","new_heading":"Step by step Struts2 form binding validation and annotation mapping guide","new_body":"<h2>Why annotations and not a giant XML graveyard</h2>
<p>If you like jumping between files and arguing with your IDE then keep using XML. If you prefer keeping wiring next to the code that does the work then annotations are your friend. Struts2 annotations place action mappings near the action class and make form handling and validation less like a scavenger hunt.</p>

<h2>Quick project setup without the drama</h2>
<p>Use Maven or Gradle to pull in Struts core and commons dependencies. Let the build tool handle versions and the classpath so you can waste your brain cells on debugging real problems later. A typical setup just needs the Struts2 core and the validation support on the classpath.</p>

<h2>Create a form bean and action class</h2>
<p>Keep form fields as plain Java properties with getters and setters. Struts2 will populate action properties that match form input names automatically when the form posts. That means no reflection rituals from the dark ages.</p>

<h3>Action class skeleton</h3>
<pre><code>public class SaveAction extends ActionSupport
{
    private String name

    public String getName() { return name }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name }

    // mapping and results will go here
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Add annotation based action mappings</h2>
<p>Drop the XML mapping and add annotations on the action class or its methods. The mappings stay close to the logic which reduces context switching and the urge to scream. Basic examples look like the snippets below.</p>

<pre><code>@Action(value = "save", results = {
    @Result(name = "success", location = "/success.jsp"),
    @Result(name = "input", location = "/form.jsp")
})
public String execute()
{
    return SUCCESS
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Handle submission and validation like a human</h2>
<p>You can validate with annotations from the validation framework or by overriding the validate method on the action. Both bind errors back to the form so the user does not have to guess what went wrong.</p>

<h3>In action validate method</h3>
<pre><code>public void validate()
{
    if (name == null || name.trim().isEmpty())
    {
        addFieldError("name", "Name is required")
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Define result views and keep paths sensible</h2>
<p>Map logical names such as success and input to JSPs or other view templates using the annotation results. Keep view paths consistent and avoid hard coded references littered across classes. Logical names are your friend for testing and refactoring.</p>

<h2>Test the flow and fix the weird cases</h2>
<p>Try the happy path and the broken path with validation errors. If you land on a page you did not expect check parameter names, action mapping values and result locations. Turn on logging to trace dispatches when the app behaves like it has a grudge against you.</p>

<h2>Checklist to avoid common traps</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Confirm form input names match action property names</li>
    <li>Verify annotation value matches the URL or action name you post to</li>
    <li>Use success and input as logical result names for predictable flow</li>
    <li>Keep validation messages tied to fields for better UX</li>
    <li>Run both happy path and validation error scenarios when testing</li>
</ul>

<h2>Parting advice that is actually useful</h2>
<p>Annotations tidy configuration and speed up development. Validation can live on the action or as annotations depending on your taste for centralized rules versus local convenience. Either way the core steps stay the same bind form fields to action properties map results to views and validate input before committing side effects.</p>

<p>Follow the steps above and you will have a Struts2 form handling flow that behaves like a professional instead of a mystery puzzle. If that fails at least you will have better logs and sharper sarcasm to share with your team.</p>"}

