  "new_title": "Clean Struts 2 exception handling with annotations and logs",
  "new_heading": "Annotation based exception mappings in Struts 2 for clean error flows",
  "new_body": "<p>Welcome to the exciting world of error handling, where your users get friendly messages and your logs get juicy context. This guide shows how to keep action classes tidy with Struts2 annotation based exception handling, map exceptions to results, log enough to be useful, and test the whole sad circus so you do not ship stack traces to customers.</p>

<h2>Why use annotation based exception mappings</h2>
<p>If you like less XML clutter and more code proximity, annotations are your friend. Annotating actions or packages with exception mappings keeps the mapping near the code it affects. That makes maintenance less painful, and you might even feel like you are slightly organized.</p>
<p>Core wins to remember</p>
<ul>
  <li>Centralize error responses so actions can focus on business logic</li>
  <li>Avoid try catch everywhere, let the framework do the plumbing</li>
  <li>Map specific exception types to named results to avoid hiding real bugs</li>
</ul>

<h3>Add the exception interceptor to your stack</h3>
<p>Struts2 uses an exception interceptor to turn thrown exceptions into result names. Make sure your interceptor stack includes it. Many projects inherit from struts default stacks that already include this interceptor, but do verify your stack loads it.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;package name="app" extends="struts-default"&gt;
  &lt;interceptor-stack name="appStack"&gt;
    &lt;interceptor-ref name="exception" /&gt;
    &lt;interceptor-ref name="defaultStack" /&gt;
  &lt;/interceptor-stack&gt;
&lt;/package&gt;
</code></pre>

<h3>Annotate actions or packages with exception mappings</h3>
<p>Apply mappings at the action level if the behavior is unique, or at the package level if many actions should share the same response. The annotations are easy to read and keep mapping close to the code that throws the exception.</p>
<pre><code>import com.opensymphony.xwork2.ActionSupport;
import org.apache.struts2.convention.annotation.ExceptionMapping;
import org.apache.struts2.convention.annotation.ExceptionMappings;
import org.apache.struts2.convention.annotation.Action;

@ExceptionMappings({
  @ExceptionMapping(exceptionName="java.lang.IllegalArgumentException", result="badInput"),
  @ExceptionMapping(exceptionName="java.lang.Exception", result="error")
})
public class MyAction extends ActionSupport {
  @Action("doSomething")
  public String execute() throws Exception {
    // business logic
    return SUCCESS;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That example maps IllegalArgumentException to a friendly badInput view and maps all other exceptions to a generic error result. Prefer specific exception classes rather than a catch all, because masking real bugs is how tickets become mysterious production gremlins.</p>

<h3>Define matching result pages</h3>
<p>Make sure the result names used in mappings match your JSP or other view results. Returning a result name from an action will trigger the mapped view. Keep user facing messages concise and avoid exposing stack traces or sensitive data.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;result name="error"&gt;/WEB INF/views/error.jsp&lt;/result&gt;
&lt;result name="badInput"&gt;/WEB INF/views/badInput.jsp&lt;/result&gt;
</code></pre>

<h3>Logging and correlation ids</h3>
<p>Throwing things at the user does not help debugging. Log exceptions with context such as action name, parameters, and user id when available. Add a correlation id to both the response header and your logs so you can trace a single request across services without guessing.</p>
<pre><code>private static final org.slf4j.Logger log = org.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger(MyAction.class);

try {
  // risky stuff
} catch (Exception e) {
  String cid = request.getHeader("X Correlation Id");
  log.error("action=doSomething cid=" + cid + " params=" + paramsMap, e);
  throw e; // let the interceptor map to a result
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Testing and best practices</h3>
<p>Automated tests should simulate exception flows and assert that the user sees a clean page while logs contain the details. Keep these rules in mind</p>
<ul>
  <li>Map specific exceptions when possible to avoid hiding bugs</li>
  <li>Reuse a small set of error results for consistency</li>
  <li>Do not show stack traces to users, show friendly messages and a correlation id</li>
  <li>Log context rich information and an id to trace the request later</li>
  <li>Write unit and integration tests that assert the correct result is returned for thrown exceptions</li>
</ul>

<h2>Wrap up</h2>
<p>Annotation based exception handling in Struts2 keeps your MVC actions focused on business logic while the framework handles mapping exceptions to results. Configure the exception interceptor, annotate actions or packages, create matching results, log smartly, and test the flows. Follow these practices and your error pages will be civil, your logs will be useful, and your oncall nights will be slightly less tragic.</p>"
}

