  "new_title": "Build a reliable Spring Boot file upload REST endpoint",
  "new_heading": "Make a multipart file upload endpoint in Spring Boot that handles errors well",
  "new_body": "<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>If your plan for file uploads is to toss files at the server and hope for the best you are in the wrong place. This guide walks through a RESTful Spring Boot multipart file upload endpoint that behaves itself. We accept a MultipartFile in a controller, hand it to a storage service, set multipart limits, add sane error handling, and test with curl or Postman. No rituals required.</p>

<h2>Quick plan</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Create a Spring Boot app with Web and multipart support</li>
  <li>Add a REST controller that accepts MultipartFile</li>
  <li>Implement a storage service that writes files to disk using unique names</li>
  <li>Configure multipart limits and add exception handling</li>
  <li>Test with curl and Postman and confirm responses</li>
</ul>

<h2>Controller</h2>
<p>Keep the controller minimal. Validate the upload and return a created response with a Location header. The controller below delegates storage to a service and returns a small JSON payload with the filename.</p>

<pre><code>@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class FileUploadController {

    private final StorageService storageService;

    public FileUploadController(StorageService storageService) {
        this.storageService = storageService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/upload")
    public ResponseEntity<?> uploadFile(@RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) throws IOException {
        if (file.isEmpty()) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("No file uploaded");
        }
        String filename = storageService.store(file);
        URI location = ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentContextPath()
            .path("/files/")
            .path(filename)
            .build()
            .toUri();
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(Collections.singletonMap("filename", filename));
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Storage service</h3>
<p>Separate storage logic from the controller. This example writes to disk and generates a UUID based filename to avoid collisions.</p>

<pre><code>public interface StorageService {
    String store(MultipartFile file) throws IOException;
}

public class FileSystemStorageService implements StorageService {

    private final Path root = Paths.get("uploads");

    public FileSystemStorageService() throws IOException {
        Files.createDirectories(root);
    }

    @Override
    public String store(MultipartFile file) throws IOException {
        String ext = StringUtils.getFilenameExtension(file.getOriginalFilename());
        String filename = UUID.randomUUID().toString() + (ext != null ? "." + ext : "");
        Path target = root.resolve(filename);
        try (InputStream in = file.getInputStream()) {
            Files.copy(in, target, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
        }
        return filename;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Configure multipart limits</h2>
<p>Set sensible defaults in application.properties so you do not get surprised by 500 errors when someone uploads a 500 MB video because they "forgot" the rules.</p>

<pre><code>spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=10MB
spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size=10MB
</code></pre>

<h2>Handle errors</h2>
<p>Translate exceptions into helpful HTTP responses. For example handle MaxUploadSizeExceededException and return 413 Payload Too Large. Your users will appreciate the hint and your logs will be less dramatic.</p>

<pre><code>@ControllerAdvice
public class RestExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(MaxUploadSizeExceededException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<?> handleMaxUpload(MaxUploadSizeExceededException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE)
            .body("File too large");
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Test with curl or Postman</h2>
<p>Use curl for scripting and Postman for manual checks. Make sure the request is multipart form data and inspect the response codes and headers.</p>

<pre><code>curl -v -F "file=@/path/to/image.jpg" http://localhost:8080/api/upload
</code></pre>

<h2>Tips and common pitfalls</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Always validate file presence and content type. Blind trust in uploads is how you get surprises.</li>
  <li>Set max file sizes early and test with a range of payloads</li>
  <li>Use UUID filenames or date based folders to avoid collisions</li>
  <li>Log request ids and responses to make debugging with curl or Postman boring and fast</li>
</ul>

<p>Follow these steps and you will have a predictable RESTful Spring file upload endpoint. If it still breaks you can blame the network gremlins and then read the logs like a responsible adult.</p>
"
}

{"new_title":"Use java.util.Scanner for Java console input and common fixes","new_heading":"Create and use Scanner objects to read from System.in in Java","new_body":"<h2>Quick overview</h2>
<p>Yes you need to import java.util.Scanner to read user input in Java. No it is not magical. The typical flow is simple and stubbornly useful. Add the import, create a Scanner that reads System.in when you want console input, call the right next method for your data type, and mind resource management so you do not surprise yourself later.</p>

<h2>Why import java.util.Scanner</h2>
<p>The Java compiler does not guess which Scanner you mean. If you do not add the import you get a compiler error about Scanner not being found. If you name one of your classes Scanner you will hide the standard one and enjoy a lovely afternoon debugging. The correct import looks like this in your source file.</p>

<pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;</code></pre>

<h2>Create a Scanner for console input</h2>
<p>Point the Scanner at System.in when you want input typed by a human or a very convincing script. Creating the object is straightforward.</p>

<pre><code>Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);</code></pre>

<p>Use nextLine to read a full line of text and nextInt to read integers. Be mindful that nextInt does not consume the trailing newline. That leftover newline is the Java equivalent of a plot twist.</p>

<pre><code>System.out.print("Name? ");
String name = scanner.nextLine();
System.out.print("Age? ");
int age = scanner.nextInt();
scanner.nextLine(); // consume the leftover newline after nextInt
</code></pre>

<h2>Read input safely and predictably</h2>
<p>Pick the right method for the job. Use nextLine for free text and nextInt nextDouble or nextBoolean for primitive types. If you mix nextInt with nextLine expect surprises. A common pattern is to call an extra nextLine after numeric input to clear the buffer.</p>

<h3>Example for a small interactive program</h3>
<pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter name: ");
        String name = scanner.nextLine();
        System.out.print("Enter age: ");
        int age = scanner.nextInt();
        scanner.nextLine(); // clear newline before nextLine calls
        System.out.println("Hello " + name + " age " + age);
        // do not close scanner here if you still need System.in later
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>When to close Scanner</h2>
<p>If your Scanner wraps a file or network stream prefer try with resources so Java will close it for you automatically. If the Scanner wraps System.in avoid closing it early if other code needs input later. Closing System.in is like locking the front door when half the guests have not left yet.</p>

<pre><code>try (Scanner fileScanner = new Scanner(new java.io.File("data.txt"))) {
    while (fileScanner.hasNextLine()) {
        String line = fileScanner.nextLine();
        // process line
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Common errors and how to fix them</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Compiler says Scanner cannot be resolved. Add import java.util.Scanner at the top or let your IDE add it for you.</li>
    <li>Your own Scanner class is hiding java.util.Scanner. Rename your class or use the fully qualified name java.util.Scanner to disambiguate.</li>
    <li>Input seems skipped after reading an int or double. Call scanner.nextLine to consume the leftover newline before reading a string.</li>
    <li>Accidentally closing System.in makes future reads fail. Only close the Scanner when you are done with all console input or when it wraps a distinct resource such as a file.</li>
</ul>

<h2>IDE tips</h2>
<p>Most IDEs will suggest imports or add them automatically. Use Organize Imports or the quick fix to add java.util.Scanner. If your IDE keeps adding the wrong import check for name clashes in your project.</p>

<h2>Recap with attitude</h2>
<p>Import java.util.Scanner, new up a Scanner with System.in, choose nextLine or nextInt depending on whether you want words or numbers, mind the leftover newline, and close resources when they are not System.in. That is the routine you will repeat until you forget and Google it again. Welcome to programming.</p>"}

{"new_title":"Make Java dialogs with JOptionPane that actually behave","new_heading":"Step by step examples for message input and confirm dialogs in Swing","new_body":"<p>If you need a tiny GUI to nag the user or collect a single text field without building a whole Swing cathedral, JOptionPane is your friend whether it knows it or not. This quick and slightly smug guide shows how to use Java JOptionPane for message dialogs input dialogs confirm dialogs and small utilities where a full GUI would be overkill</p>

<h2>Show a basic message dialog</h2>
<p>Want to flash an alert and move on with your life Use the simplest API and do not overthink it</p>
<pre><code>JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(null, "Hello from JOptionPane")</code></pre>
<p>This is the fastest way to report status to a user who did not ask for a log file</p>

<h2>Prompt for text input with an input dialog</h2>
<p>Collect a single value without building a form. Remember the user can cancel or close the window so the return value can be null</p>
<pre><code>String name = JOptionPane.showInputDialog(null, "Enter name")
if (name == null) {
    // user cancelled or closed the dialog
} else {
    // safe to use name here
}</code></pre>

<h2>Ask for confirmation with a confirm dialog</h2>
<p>When you need a yes or no decision the confirm dialog returns an int you compare to constants like JOptionPane.YES_OPTION or JOptionPane.NO_OPTION</p>
<pre><code>int choice = JOptionPane.showConfirmDialog(null, "Continue?")
if (choice == JOptionPane.YES_OPTION) {
    // go ahead
} else if (choice == JOptionPane.NO_OPTION) {
    // do not proceed
} else {
    // dialog closed or cancelled
}</code></pre>

<h2>Customize buttons and icons with an option dialog</h2>
<p>For anything not covered by the simple helpers use showOptionDialog to supply your own button labels icons and the full kitchen sink of options</p>
<pre><code>Object[] options = {"Save", "Discard"}
int pick = JOptionPane.showOptionDialog(null, "Save changes", "Confirm", JOptionPane.DEFAULT_OPTION, JOptionPane.QUESTION_MESSAGE, null, options, options[0])
if (pick == 0) {
    // Save chosen
} else if (pick == 1) {
    // Discard chosen
} else {
    // closed window
}</code></pre>

<h3>Notes on safe handling</h3>
<ul>
<li>Always check for null from input dialogs to handle cancel and close events gracefully</li>
<li>Compare confirm and option results to JOptionPane constants to avoid magic numbers</li>
<li>Do not assume a dialog returned a valid string or index if the user closed the window</li>
</ul>

<h2>Threading and the event dispatch thread</h2>
<p>Dialogs are Swing components so call them on the event dispatch thread to avoid weird freezing or race conditions. For example use SwingUtilities.invokeLater to schedule dialog calls when you are not already on the EDT</p>

<h2>When to use JOptionPane and when to stop</h2>
<p>JOptionPane is perfect for debugging tools quick utilities and throwaway GUIs. If your UI has many fields validation rules or complex flows build a proper Swing form instead. Think of JOptionPane as the paper towel of GUIs it soaks up small spills and then disappears</p>

<h3>Quick pitfalls and tips</h3>
<ul>
<li>Do not bury important logic in a dialog response without handling cancel and close</li>
<li>Keep messages short and clear or users will start clicking randomly</li>
<li>Use option dialogs when you need custom labels or non standard flows</li>
</ul>

<p>This guide covered message dialog usage input dialogs confirm dialogs and option dialogs with short examples and safe handling tips for Java Swing using JOptionPane. Now go forth and pop sensible dialogs instead of mysterious ones</p>"}

{"new_title":"Get Hibernate and JPA3 Working in Java without Tears or Drama","new_heading":"Step by step Hibernate JPA3 persistence setup for Java applications","new_body":"<h2>Why you need this guide</h2>
<p>If your app needs persistence and you like your data intact the Hibernate JPA3 combo is a solid choice. This guide walks through the essentials of wiring up Hibernate with the JPA3 API in a Java project. It is practical, a little sarcastic, and focused on the parts that actually break in production.</p>

<h2>Dependencies and build setup</h2>
<p>Add the ORM library and the Jakarta persistence API into your build. If you use Spring you can swap manual wiring for a Spring adapter and avoid reinventing the dependency wheel.</p>

<p>Example Maven dependencies that you will need</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.hibernate.orm&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;hibernate-core&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;6.2.0.Final&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;

&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;jakarta.persistence&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;jakarta.persistence-api&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;3.0.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;

&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.postgresql&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;postgresql&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;42.5.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>If you use Gradle just add the corresponding artifacts to your implementation configuration. If you are on a managed container you may not need the provider jar on the classpath.</p>

<h2>Define the persistence unit</h2>
<p>Create a persistence unit name you will remember and reference it when creating the EntityManagerFactory. You can use a persistence.xml file or configure properties programmatically with the Persistence API.</p>

<pre><code>&lt;persistence&gt;
  &lt;persistence-unit name="myUnit"&gt;
    &lt;provider&gt;org.hibernate.jpa.HibernatePersistenceProvider&lt;/provider&gt;
    &lt;properties&gt;
      &lt;property name="jakarta.persistence.jdbc.driver" value="org.postgresql.Driver"/&gt;
      &lt;property name="jakarta.persistence.jdbc.url" value="your jdbc url here"/&gt;
      &lt;property name="jakarta.persistence.jdbc.user" value="db user"/&gt;
      &lt;property name="hibernate.dialect" value="org.hibernate.dialect.PostgreSQLDialect"/&gt;
    &lt;/properties&gt;
  &lt;/persistence-unit&gt;
&lt;/persistence&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Tip do not leave production credentials or schema auto update turned on when you push to prod. That is how databases die a slow and confusing death.</p>

<h2>Define your entities the right way</h2>
<p>Annotate domain classes with @Entity and pick a stable primary key with @Id. Keep mapping concerns separate from business logic when possible. Use attribute converters or embeddables for small complex fields instead of stuffing everything into one giant table friendly blob.</p>

<p>Keep these rules in mind</p>
<ul>
  <li>Annotate identity fields explicitly</li>
  <li>Favor constructor based creation for required fields</li>
  <li>Lazy load collections and be mindful of session boundaries</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bootstrapping the EntityManagerFactory</h2>
<p>Outside a container create an EntityManagerFactory once and reuse it across threads. Use Persistence.createEntityManagerFactory with your persistence unit name or let the container provide an EMF. Creating and closing factories like they are cheap is a classic source of connection leaks and performance pain.</p>

<p>When using Spring you can replace manual EMF creation with a LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean or use Spring Data JPA for repositories and less boilerplate.</p>

<h3>Lifecycle and threading</h3>
<p>Share the EntityManagerFactory, but do not share EntityManager instances across threads. Open a new EntityManager per unit of work and close it when you are done. Always bound transactions explicitly and verify that rollbacks and commits behave as expected.</p>

<h2>Smoke test and debugging tips</h2>
<p>Write a tiny test that persists a simple entity and reads it back. Check transaction boundaries, verify lazy loading behavior, and watch for N plus one queries that sneak in when you are not looking.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Enable SQL logging with the Hibernate properties for show sql and format sql to see what the ORM actually does</li>
  <li>Use a schema update tool only during development to avoid surprises in staging and production</li>
  <li>Log parameters and exceptions when queries misbehave to speed up diagnosis</li>
</ul>

<h2>Final checklist before you call it done</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Dependencies are declared and available at runtime</li>
  <li>Persistence unit is named and reachable from your bootstrap code</li>
  <li>Entities use correct annotations and mapping rules</li>
  <li>EntityManagerFactory is created once and shared</li>
  <li>SQL logging is enabled in development for troubleshooting</li>
</ul>

<p>If you follow these steps your Java project will boot a working Hibernate JPA3 persistence layer that behaves well enough for local development and early testing. If something breaks refer to the logs and blame your configuration gently. Then fix it.</p>"}

{"new_title":"Make a Java AWS Lambda with Maven and deploy quickly","new_heading":"Complete Java Lambda handler setup with Maven packaging and AWS deployment walkthrough","new_body":"<p>So you want a Java AWS Lambda that actually runs and does not make you cry. Good news The world of serverless is mostly painless once you stop expecting magic and start copying a few sensible steps. This guide walks you from a bare Maven project to a runnable Lambda jar uploaded in the AWS Console with a quick test and CloudWatch proof of life.</p>

<h2>Quick overview</h2>
<p>High level version for humans in a hurry</p>
<ul>
  <li>Create a Maven project and add the AWS Lambda libraries</li>
  <li>Write a handler that implements RequestHandler or a simple handleRequest method</li>
  <li>Build a fat jar with the Maven shade plugin</li>
  <li>Create the function in the AWS Console and upload the jar</li>
  <li>Run a test event and read CloudWatch logs to see what you broke</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 1 Create a Maven project</h2>
<p>Use your usual Maven archetype or a blank project and pick group and artifact ids that do not make your team regret life choices later. Add these dependencies so the Lambda runtime can call your handler and so you can accept common event objects.</p>

<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.amazonaws&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;aws-lambda-java-core&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;1.2.1&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;

&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.amazonaws&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;aws-lambda-java-events&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;3.11.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Exact versions change over time so pick the current stable ones when you build. If you only need the core runtime leave out events until you need them.</p>

<h2>Step 2 Write a handler class</h2>
<p>Keep it tiny for the first run. The runtime expects either an implementation of RequestHandler or a public method named handleRequest with the right signature. Example that does not lie about its job follows.</p>

<pre><code>package com.example.lambda;

import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.Context;
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.RequestHandler;

public class HelloHandler implements RequestHandler&lt;String,String&gt; {
    @Override
    public String handleRequest(String input, Context context) {
        context.getLogger().log("Input received " + input);
        return "Hello " + (input == null ? "world" : input);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>If you want typed events use the classes from aws-lambda-java-events. For simple tests a String in and String out keeps things readable and boring in the best possible way.</p>

<h2>Step 3 Build a deployable jar</h2>
<p>Lambda wants a single jar with all dependencies if you are not using layers. Use the Maven shade plugin or assembly to create a fat jar. Add the plugin to your build and run mvn package.</p>

<pre><code>mvn clean package
# check target for your-artifact-id-version.jar
</code></pre>

<p>If the jar is not fat your function will fail at runtime with a ClassNotFoundException and the logs will be brutally honest about your mistakes.</p>

<h2>Step 4 Create the Lambda in the AWS Console</h2>
<p>Go to the AWS Console, choose Create function and pick Java 11 or Java 17 runtime depending on what you built. Pick an execution role that has permissions for CloudWatch Logs. A basic role with logging rights is fine for testing.</p>

<h2>Step 5 Upload the jar and set the handler</h2>
<p>Upload the fat jar from target. In the handler field provide the full class name and method mapping using the double colon style like this</p>

<pre><code>com.example.lambda.HelloHandler::handleRequest
</code></pre>

<p>Configure memory and timeout. For a simple hello world a small memory size and a short timeout work. For real work bump those until performance stops whining.</p>

<h2>Step 6 Test and read the logs</h2>
<p>Use the test event editor to give a minimal JSON payload and click Test. The response will appear inline but the real story is in CloudWatch Logs where your context.getLogger output and stack traces live. Those logs are the place where you learn humility and how to fix your handler.</p>

<h3>Troubleshooting quick fixes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>ClassNotFoundException on invoke Build a fat jar and reupload</li>
  <li>Method not found Check the handler string and confirm the method signature</li>
  <li>No logs Check the execution role has logs Put a throwaway log immediately at the start of your handler</li>
</ul>

<h2>Wrap up and next steps</h2>
<p>Congratulations You now have a working Java Lambda deployed from Maven to the AWS Console and a CloudWatch log trail to prove it. Next, consider using layers to share libraries, switch to CI CD to upload automatically, or explore serverless frameworks if you want your deployments to stop being manual rituals.</p>

<p>If you enjoyed this tiny tour of the Java Lambda lifecycle you are ready for event sources such as S3 or API Gateway and the inevitable debugging that follows. Welcome to the serverless club where functions are cheap and curiosity is expensive.</p>"}

