  "new_title": "Enable Java 21 preview features the smart and painless way",
  "new_heading": "Get Java 21 preview features compiling running and testing with Maven or Gradle",
  "new_body": "<p>So you want to try Java 21 preview features without detonating your build or pride. Good news, it is not rocket surgery. This guide walks through installing JDK 21 enabling preview at compile time and runtime and wiring up Maven or Gradle so tests behave the same way as your local runs. Keep an experimental mindset and a backup branch.</p>

<h2>Install JDK 21</h2>
<p>Download a JDK 21 build from a trusted vendor and set JAVA_HOME to that installation. Make sure the distribution you choose includes support for preview features. Command line tools must come from the same JDK or you will see odd errors and sad developers.</p>

<h2>Compile with preview enabled</h2>
<p>When compiling tell the compiler you mean business and want preview language and API checks. Use the release flag plus enable preview so the compiler produces class files that expect the same runtime flags.</p>
<pre><code>javac --release 21 --enable-preview MyApp.java</code></pre>
<p>This makes javac validate preview syntax and produce class files tied to the preview mode. If you skip this you will get compile or runtime surprises.</p>

<h2>Run with preview enabled</h2>
<p>The JVM also needs the preview flag. Running without it will usually fail with parse errors or unsupported class version messages when preview syntax or APIs are present.</p>
<pre><code>java --enable-preview MyApp</code></pre>

<h2>Configure Maven</h2>
<p>Add compiler args to the maven compiler plugin and make sure your test plugins pass the same runtime flag. A minimal example looks like this.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;plugin&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.maven.plugins&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;maven-compiler-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;3.11.0&lt;/version&gt;
  &lt;configuration&gt;
    &lt;release&gt;21&lt;/release&gt;
    &lt;compilerArgs&gt;
      &lt;arg&gt;--enable-preview&lt;/arg&gt;
    &lt;/compilerArgs&gt;
  &lt;/configuration&gt;
&lt;/plugin&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Then pass the runtime flag to surefire or failsafe so tests run the same way.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;plugin&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.maven.plugins&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;maven-surefire-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;configuration&gt;
    &lt;argLine&gt;--enable-preview&lt;/argLine&gt;
  &lt;/configuration&gt;
&lt;/plugin&gt;</code></pre>

<h2>Configure Gradle</h2>
<p>For Gradle add compiler args and runtime args on the relevant tasks so both compilation and tests use preview mode. Example for a Groovy build script.</p>
<pre><code>tasks.withType(JavaCompile) {
    options.compilerArgs += ['--release', '21', '--enable-preview']
}

tasks.test {
    jvmArgs += ['--enable-preview']
}</code></pre>

<h2>Test and avoid surprises in production</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Run unit tests and CI with the exact same flags used to compile and run locally so behavior matches.</li>
  <li>Remember that preview features can change between JDK updates. Do not ship preview code to production without a migration plan.</li>
  <li>Keep an eye on the relevant JEPs for details and compatibility notes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Quick tip</h3>
<p>Always pass --enable-preview to both the compiler and the JVM and add the same flag to test tasks. That single habit saves hours of debugging and a few mild swear words.</p>

<p>There you go. You can now try out Java 21 preview features with javac Maven and Gradle and still sleep at night. Just treat preview code as experimental and be ready to refactor when the JEPs move things around.</p>"
}

{"new_title":"Get Jenkins CI Working Today with Practical Pipeline Steps","new_heading":"A hands on Jenkinsfile and pipeline tutorial for CI in DevOps teams","new_body":"<h2>Why bother with Jenkins if you like manual toil</h2>
<p>If you are new to Jenkins or Continuous Integration and you enjoy spending evenings pushing builds by hand then this guide will ruin your hobby. For everyone else Jenkins gives you automated builds pipeline as code and repeatable CI that plays nicely with Git and your DevOps workflow. This is a practical beginner friendly tutorial that walks through install basic setup a Declarative Pipeline and connecting webhooks for automated runs.</p>

<h2>Quick overview of what we will do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Install Jenkins on a server or run it in Docker</li>
  <li>Complete the initial setup and add recommended plugins</li>
  <li>Add credentials and configure global tools like JDK Maven and Docker</li>
  <li>Create a Declarative Pipeline in a Jenkinsfile and test builds</li>
  <li>Connect a Git repository and add webhooks for automation</li>
</ul>

<h2>Install Jenkins and get to the web UI</h2>
<p>Install Jenkins on Linux with the official package or use the Jenkins docker image for a predictable environment. After install open the Jenkins web UI on port 8080 and follow the setup wizard to unlock the instance and install recommended plugins. Yes that wizard will ask for an admin user and a password that lives in a file so copy it and move on.</p>

<h2>Initial configuration and plugin selection</h2>
<p>The setup wizard covers most common plugins but you will likely add more for GitHub GitLab pipeline utilities and build tools. Add credentials to the Jenkins credentials store for Git access and any build servers. Then tell Jenkins where your global tools live such as JDK Maven and Docker so pipeline steps do not all have to guess the JDK version.</p>

<h2>Create a Declarative Pipeline with a Jenkinsfile</h2>
<p>Store a Jenkinsfile in your project repository and treat it like source code. Declarative Pipeline syntax is friendly for beginners and readable for everyone else. Define stages for checkout build test and deploy and reuse shared libraries for logic you do not want to rewrite four times.</p>

<pre><code>pipeline {
  agent any
  stages {
    stage('Checkout') {
      steps {
        checkout scm
      }
    }
    stage('Build') {
      steps {
        sh 'mvn -B -DskipTests package'
      }
    }
    stage('Test') {
      steps {
        sh 'mvn test'
      }
    }
    stage('Deploy') {
      steps {
        echo 'Deploy step here'
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

<h3>Notes about the Jenkinsfile</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keep the Jenkinsfile in the same repo as your code for reproducible pipelines</li>
  <li>Start with simple stages and add complexity as you gain confidence</li>
  <li>Use shared libraries when multiple projects need the same logic</li>
</ul>

<h2>Connect a Git repository and enable webhooks</h2>
<p>Add your repository URL and credentials to your job or use Multibranch Pipeline to let Jenkins discover branches and pull requests automatically. Configure webhooks on GitHub or GitLab so pushes and PR events trigger a build. Multibranch saves you from making a million nearly identical jobs which is a nice change from the dark ages.</p>

<h2>Run builds monitor logs and fix what breaks</h2>
<p>You can run a build manually or push a change to trigger automation. Watch the console logs for failures and adjust stages as needed. Console logs are blunt but effective truth machines. Keep build steps idempotent and avoid relying on ephemeral local state.</p>

<h2>Post build notifications and feedback loops</h2>
<p>Configure post build notifications to Slack email or other channels so the team knows when a build greets success or sputters. Fast feedback keeps bugs small and arguments fewer. Use credential binding for secret values and never commit passwords to Git unless you like trouble.</p>

<h2>Scaling and next steps</h2>
<p>When one Jenkins controller is not enough add agents that run jobs on dedicated nodes or in containers. Use pipeline libraries to centralize shared logic and consider Blue Ocean or other UI plugins if you want prettier build graphs. For heavy workloads look at distributed builds and build caching strategies.</p>

<h2>Recap and a few pro tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Install Jenkins via package or Docker and open the UI on port 8080</li>
  <li>Use the setup wizard and install recommended plugins</li>
  <li>Store a Jenkinsfile in your repo and prefer Declarative Pipeline for clarity</li>
  <li>Use Multibranch Pipeline for automatic branch and PR builds</li>
  <li>Add credentials to the credentials store and use binding for secrets</li>
</ul>

<p>Jenkins is not magic but it is powerful automation for CI and DevOps. Treat your pipelines as code add tests and keep builds fast. Now go create a pipeline and pretend you did not enjoy the debugger ride.</p>"}

{"new_title":"Clean up your Java List fast with LinkedHashSet and Streams","new_heading":"Smart ways to remove duplicates from a Java List while keeping order","new_body":"<h1>Smart ways to remove duplicates from a Java List while keeping order</h1>
<p>So you have a Java List full of déjà vu entries and you want to remove duplicates without starting a full database crusade. Lucky for you Java offers a tidy handful of options that balance readability, performance and the occasional need to log dramatic messages when duplicates are found.</p>

<h2>Quick cheat sheet</h2>
<ul>
  <li>LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order and is simple to use</li>
  <li>Streams distinct gives compact code and keeps encounter order</li>
  <li>A manual loop with HashSet gives total control and room for side effects</li>
  <li>TreeSet deduplicates while sorting, but it will reorder your list</li>
  <li>Custom objects need equals and hashCode so sets can tell clones apart</li>
</ul>

<h2>Use LinkedHashSet when order matters</h2>
<p>If you want to keep the original insertion order and you do not need anything fancy just convert to a LinkedHashSet and back. It is simple and fast enough for most everyday lists.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "a");
List<String> unique = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(list));
</code></pre>

<p>Pros include predictable iteration order and O(n) average time. The downside is extra memory to hold the set copy. If that bothers you then consider streaming or in place filtering.</p>

<h2>Use Streams distinct for tidy pipelines</h2>
<p>Streams are the stylish choice. They keep code concise and preserve encounter order while removing duplicates. It is also easy to insert other operations in the pipeline without turning your method into a horror story.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>List<String> unique = list.stream()
    .distinct()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
</code></pre>

<p>Under the hood distinct uses a stateful set to track seen elements so expect similar memory behavior to LinkedHashSet. Streams shine when your logic is already fluent and you like sidewalks over mud.</p>

<h2>Use a manual loop with HashSet when you want control</h2>
<p>Want to log each duplicate, trigger side effects or apply custom filtering while deduping Then write the loop yourself. This is the most explicit approach and it does not hide behavior behind clever one liners.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();
List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
for (String val : list) {
    if (seen.add(val)) {
        result.add(val);
    } else {
        // duplicate detected, do something if needed
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>HashSet gives average O(1) insertion and lookup. Use LinkedHashSet instead if you need to keep order and still have that explicit loop vibe.</p>

<h2>Use TreeSet when sorting plus deduping is wanted</h2>
<p>If sorted output is the point then TreeSet will both sort and remove duplicates. Be warned this changes insertion order and costs O(n log n) time.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>List<String> sortedUnique = new ArrayList<>(new TreeSet<>(list));
</code></pre>

<p>If you are working with custom objects make sure they implement Comparable or provide a Comparator to the TreeSet or you will get an exception and then the kind of blank stare programmers learn to give the stack trace.</p>

<h2>When using custom types remember equals and hashCode</h2>
<p>Hash based collections rely on equals and hashCode to detect duplicates. If your objects do not override these methods equal objects will not be treated as duplicates.</p>

<h3>Minimal example</h3>
<pre><code>public class Person {
    private final String id;
    private final String name;

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
        Person person = (Person) o;
        return Objects.equals(id, person.id);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(id);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Make equals and hashCode consistent and your sets will be able to do their job without therapy.</p>

<h2>Performance notes and final recommendation</h2>
<p>For general purpose deduplication use LinkedHashSet or Streams distinct. They keep order and are easy to read. Use a manual loop with HashSet when you need logging or special side effects. Reach for TreeSet only when you want sorted results and accept the higher cost.</p>

<p>If memory matters consider streaming approaches that process lazily if you can avoid building large intermediate collections. Always profile before optimizing unless you enjoy guessing games.</p>

<h2>Recap</h2>
<p>Five practical options were covered to remove duplicates from a Java List while balancing order and performance. Pick the one that fits your needs and move on to the next bug. Life is short and lists are long.</p>"}

{"new_title":"Turn Java strings into ints and longs without drama","new_heading":"Practical parsing tips for Java String to int and String to long","new_body":"<p>So you have a Java String and you want an int or a long. Welcome to the thrilling world of parsing where bad input will ruin your day. This quick guide gives parsing tips to convert a Java String to primitive types in Java while avoiding NumberFormatException and the usual debugging curse words.</p>

<h2>Quick rules to keep your code from exploding</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Use Integer.parseInt when you need a primitive int.</li>
  <li>Use Long.parseLong when values can exceed int range.</li>
  <li>Catch NumberFormatException when input is untrusted.</li>
  <li>Use valueOf when you need a wrapper like Integer for collections.</li>
  <li>Create a tryParse helper to stop copy pasting try catch everywhere.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Parsing a decimal String to int</h2>
<p>The simplest case is a well behaved decimal string. Integer.parseInt does exactly what it says. If the string is not a valid int Java throws NumberFormatException which is the language's way of saying do not feed it garbage.</p>

<pre><code>int n = Integer.parseInt("123");</code></pre>

<p>Keep this in mind. If the input contains non digits or the numeric value is outside the int range you will hit NumberFormatException. Which is fair. You fed it bad data.</p>

<h2>Parsing larger values to long</h2>
<p>When your numbers get bolder use Long.parseLong. It behaves like Integer.parseInt but for long primitive types so it covers bigger values.</p>

<pre><code>long l = Long.parseLong("1234567890123");</code></pre>

<p>Pick the primitive types based on expected magnitude. If you accidentally choose int and then receive a phone number or timestamp you did not intend to parse you will learn about integer overflow the hard way.</p>

<h2>Handle NumberFormatException gracefully</h2>
<p>Try catch is not exciting but it is effective. Wrap parsing calls that rely on user input or external data in a targeted catch block.</p>

<pre><code>try {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(s);
    // use n
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    // handle bad input, show user friendly message or fallback
}</code></pre>

<p>Use specific catch blocks so you do not accidentally mask other bugs. Logging the offending string is helpful unless you are trying to preserve privacy or hide evidence.</p>

<h2>When to use valueOf and boxed types</h2>
<p>If you need an object rather than a primitive for collections or APIs then use valueOf. It returns the wrapper type and can benefit from caching for small values.</p>

<pre><code>Integer boxed = Integer.valueOf("123");
Long boxedLong = Long.valueOf("1234567890123");</code></pre>

<p>valueOf will still throw NumberFormatException for invalid input. It is not magic, it only wraps the same parsing logic in an object.</p>

<h2>Make a tryParse helper to reduce boilerplate</h2>
<p>If you are tired of repeating try catch everywhere write a small helper. Return null or Optional to indicate invalid input depending on your style and null tolerance.</p>

<pre><code>static Integer tryParseInt(String s) {
    try {
        return Integer.parseInt(s);
    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
        return null; // or use Optional<Integer>
    }
}

static java.util.Optional<Integer> tryParseIntOptional(String s) {
    try {
        return java.util.Optional.of(Integer.parseInt(s));
    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
        return java.util.Optional.empty();
    }
}</code></pre>

<p>Use Optional when you want callers to handle absence explicitly. Use null when you are writing quick scripts and have a death wish.</p>

<h2>Recap and best practices</h2>
<p>To convert a Java String to int or long primitive types use Integer.parseInt and Long.parseLong when you trust the input. Catch NumberFormatException for untrusted data. Use valueOf when you need wrapper types. Consider a tryParse helper to make parsing code readable and reduce repeated try catch blocks.</p>

<p>Follow these parsing tips and your runtime surprises will drop, which means fewer frantic searches through stack traces at 2 a m. You are welcome.</p>"}

