  "new_title": "Node.js file uploads with Multer and JavaScript made reliable",
  "new_heading": "Step by step Node and browser file upload with Express and Multer",
  "new_body": "<article>
  <p>So you want file upload that does not implode when a user tries to upload a 200 megabyte cat photo collection. Good choice. This guide walks through a practical file upload flow using JavaScript on the client and Node.js on the backend. We use HTML5 forms and a tiny Express server with Multer to parse multipart data and keep your app from crying in production.</p>

  <h2>Setup and install dependencies</h2>
  <p>Start a project and add the essentials for this upload example. On your dev box run:</p>
  <pre><code>npm init -y
npm install express multer</code></pre>
  <p>Create a server file named <code>app.js</code> and a <code>public</code> folder for your client assets. This gives you a minimal backend and a place to throw your slightly panicked test uploads.</p>

  <h2>Client form and JavaScript</h2>
  <p>Use a simple HTML5 multipart form and let JavaScript send a FormData object so you can show progress and avoid duplicate submits. Keep the UI decisions mercifully simple.</p>
  <pre><code>&lt;form id='uploadForm' enctype='multipart/form-data'&gt;
  &lt;input type='file' name='file' id='fileInput' /&gt;
  &lt;button type='submit' id='sendBtn'&gt;Upload&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

&lt;div id='progress'&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>JavaScript that posts the file and shows progress. We use XMLHttpRequest here because fetch does not provide upload progress yet in all browsers.</p>
  <pre><code>const form = document.getElementById('uploadForm')
const fileInput = document.getElementById('fileInput')
const sendBtn = document.getElementById('sendBtn')
const progress = document.getElementById('progress')

form.addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
  e.preventDefault()
  const file = fileInput.files[0]
  if (!file) {
    progress.textContent = 'Pick a file first'
    return
  }

  sendBtn.disabled = true
  const fd = new FormData()
  fd.append('file', file)

  const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest()
  xhr.open('POST', '/upload')

  xhr.upload.onprogress = function(evt) {
    if (evt.lengthComputable) {
      const pct = Math.round(evt.loaded / evt.total * 100)
      progress.textContent = `Uploading ${pct}%`
    }
  }

  xhr.onload = function() {
    sendBtn.disabled = false
    if (xhr.status === 200) {
      progress.textContent = 'Upload complete'
      console.log('Server response', xhr.responseText)
    } else {
      progress.textContent = 'Upload failed'
    }
  }

  xhr.onerror = function() {
    sendBtn.disabled = false
    progress.textContent = 'Network error'
  }

  xhr.send(fd)
})</code></pre>

  <h2>Server with Express and Multer</h2>
  <p>Express serves the client and Multer parses multipart requests on the backend. Configure storage rules to avoid filename collisions and to keep uploads in a safe folder on disk or on a cloud bucket.</p>
  <p>A minimal app looks like this</p>
  <pre><code>const express = require('express')
const multer = require('multer')
const path = require('path')

const app = express()

// keep uploads in a directory outside the public web root
const uploadDir = path.join(__dirname, 'uploads')

// simple disk storage with unique filename
const storage = multer.diskStorage({
  destination: function(req, file, cb) {
    cb(null, uploadDir)
  },
  filename: function(req, file, cb) {
    const safeName = Date.now() + '-' + file.originalname.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9.-_]/g, '')
    cb(null, safeName)
  }
})

const upload = multer({ storage: storage, limits: { fileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024 } })

app.use(express.static('public'))

app.post('/upload', upload.single('file'), function(req, res) {
  if (!req.file) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'No file provided' })
  }
  res.json({ filename: req.file.filename, size: req.file.size })
})

app.listen(3000, function() {
  console.log('Server listening on port 3000')
})</code></pre>
  <p>This sets a 10 megabyte size limit and stores files under an uploads folder. Adjust limits and storage strategy for your environment. If you plan to serve uploads directly put them outside the public folder and serve through a controlled route that enforces auth.</p>

  <h2>Validating uploads and avoiding accident city</h2>
  <p>Never trust the client. Client side checks are polite suggestions not security measures. Implement server side validation for type and size.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Check <code>file.mimetype</code> to accept only known types like image/png and image/jpeg if you only want images</li>
    <li>Check <code>file.size</code> to reject huge payloads early</li>
    <li>Sanitize filenames and use unique IDs or timestamps to avoid overwrites</li>
    <li>Store outside the web root or use signed URLs for direct cloud uploads</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Testing and error handling</h2>
  <p>Try your flow from the browser and from the command line. A quick curl test looks like this</p>
  <pre><code>curl -F 'file=@/path/to/file' http://localhost:3000/upload</code></pre>
  <p>Handle common failure modes gracefully and return clear JSON so the client can display useful messages. Example errors to cover are missing file, invalid type, and disk write errors.</p>

  <h2>Final tips and production notes</h2>
  <p>This tutorial is a backend and frontend upload example that uses JavaScript on the client and Node.js with Express and Multer on the server. For production think about:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Streaming uploads to S3 or a blob store to avoid disk I O and local storage limits</li>
    <li>Scanning uploads for malware if you accept files from strangers</li>
    <li>Rate limiting and auth for upload endpoints to prevent abuse</li>
    <li>Clear user feedback so upload progress does not feel like punishment</li>
  </ul>

  <p>There you go. You now have a functioning file upload flow that plays nicely with modern HTML5 forms and real world Node.js backends. Go forth and accept files with caution and a healthy sense of skepticism.</p>
</article>"
}

