  "new_title": "Build a Spring Boot Multipart File Upload API That Actually Works",
  "new_heading": "Practical guide to validating and storing multipart uploads in Spring Boot",
  "new_body": "<p>If your app needs to accept files from users without turning your JVM into a meltdown simulator you are in the right place. This guide shows a practical Spring Boot REST API for multipart file upload with sensible validation file storage and testing tips that do not involve praying to the GC.</p>

<h2>Quick overview of the flow</h2>
<p>Here is what we will cover without the fluff. Client posts a multipart form to an endpoint. Controller accepts a MultipartFile. Service streams the file to disk. Validation rejects junk uploads early. Tests confirm it all behaves like a civilized API.</p>

<h2>Controller and endpoint</h2>
<p>Keep the controller tiny and honest. Let it accept a MultipartFile and return clear responses so clients know whether to celebrate or retry.</p>

<pre><code>@RestController
public class UploadController {

    @PostMapping("/upload")
    public ResponseEntity&lt;String&gt; upload(@RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) {
        if (file.isEmpty()) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("No file provided");
        }
        try {
            uploadService.save(file);
            return ResponseEntity.ok("File uploaded");
        } catch (UnsupportedFileTypeException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNSUPPORTED_MEDIA_TYPE).body(e.getMessage());
        } catch (IOException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body("Could not save file");
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Multipart settings to avoid surprise outages</h2>
<p>Put these in <code>application.properties</code> or <code>application.yml</code> so large uploads do not assassinate your app.</p>

<pre><code>spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=10MB
spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size=20MB
spring.servlet.multipart.location=/var/app/uploads
</code></pre>

<p>The <code>location</code> should be outside your deployment artifacts so a redeploy does not erase user files. Also set a sensible permanent storage location rather than relying on the temp folder if you want files to persist past the next server restart.</p>

<h2>Saving files the right way</h2>
<p>Streaming is your friend. Do not read entire files into memory unless you like OutOfMemoryError parties.</p>

<pre><code>public Path save(MultipartFile file) throws IOException {
    String filename = StringUtils.cleanPath(Objects.requireNonNull(file.getOriginalFilename()));
    Path target = storageDir.resolve(filename);
    try (InputStream in = file.getInputStream()) {
        Files.copy(in, target, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
    }
    return target;
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Storage tips</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keep uploads outside the codebase</li>
  <li>Use a unique filename or directory per user to avoid collisions</li>
  <li>Consider virus scanning or a background processing queue for heavy lifting</li>
</ul>

<h2>Validation and error handling</h2>
<p>Reject the obvious stuff early. Here are the checks we recommend.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Reject empty files with HTTP 400</li>
  <li>Enforce allowed MIME types and return 415 for bad types</li>
  <li>Check size against your max file size and return 400 or 413 accordingly</li>
  <li>Return clear messages so clients can behave like decent humans</li>
</ul>

<pre><code>List&lt;String&gt; allowed = List.of("image/png","image/jpeg","application/pdf");
if (!allowed.contains(file.getContentType())) {
    throw new UnsupportedFileTypeException("Unsupported content type");
}
if (file.getSize() == 0) {
    throw new BadRequestException("Empty file");
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Testing the endpoint</h2>
<p>Manual tests are fast and satisfying. Automated tests keep you humble.</p>

<p>Manual curl example</p>
<pre><code>curl -F "file=@my.jpg" http://localhost:8080/upload
</code></pre>

<p>Automated test ideas</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use MockMvc to post a multipart request and assert response status and message</li>
  <li>Use MockMultipartFile in unit tests to verify validation paths without touching disk</li>
  <li>Integration test that posts a real file then asserts it exists in storage</li>
</ul>

<h2>Summary and parting advice</h2>
<p>This example gives a minimal controller multipart configuration streaming storage and validation rules that keep your app sane. Build small controllers delegate saving to a service stream the uploads to disk and test both the sad paths and the sunny ones. If you follow these steps your API will accept files without staging a JVM disaster and your users will be slightly happier than before.</p>

<p>Now go write some code and try not to use the uploads folder as a trash can for 5GB video files</p>"
